nknown above: whether more seeds/recycles and more pairs would make geometry predict sorting.\n\nLet me also verify the AFDB number for a secondary citation. Quick search.\n\nc) For endosome-md: jvi.00843-21 MD details. Fetch.\n\nd) For cryoet-average: PMC11381518 already gives: \"46 3D tomograms, composed of 41 such images each, yielded 85,135 symmetry-expanded particles for sub-tomogram averaging\" and \"generating 159,660 particles\" after symmetry expansion and particle subtraction; initial map 11.0 Å from 2661 hand-picked particles. Great numbers. Resolution11 Å → then better after classification. Quantity = particles contributing to the average → resolution. Largest reported: 159,660 symmetry-expanded particles (URL PMC11381518). Unknown above: whether more particles/refinement would resolve the capsid–receptor interface at epitope-level resolution sufficient to derive binding signatures that track fate.\n\ne) For sequential: cryo-ET: \"multiple rounds of refinement\" (each iteration waits on previous); number of steps: ~hand-picking → CTF → reconstruction → alignment → classification rounds (order5-10 iterations); independent runs: per tilt-series/tomogram; GPU busy: hours per tomogram. Steps must run in order (iterative refinement), but tomograms/particles are independent across datasets — can run concurrently; nearest source that tried parallel processing at scale: the LRP6 paper's 46-tomogram pipeline (PMC11381518). OK.\n\nf) For library-ml sequential: training epochs sequential; but scoring is parallel. Actually for the \"sequential\" field for library-ml: \"none: <why the work already splits into independent pieces>\" — scoring 25M–1.7B sequences is batched parallel inference; training is a single pass over data but datasets/folds are independent. Hmm — training does have sequential epochs. The instruction: \"the part of the work that one answer waits on step after step (what each step waits for, and roughly how many steps one run takes), how many independent runs the answer needs, and how much of one GPU a single run keeps busy; then whether those steps must run in order, and if not, what change to the computation would let them run at the same time...\" For ML training: epochs are sequential (each epoch waits on previous weights); one run = training to convergence (~100s-1000s of epochs/steps); independent runs = seeds × CV folds; GPU: one GPU busy during training. Must run in order? Gradient steps are inherently sequential within a run, but different seeds/folds run at once; what would let steps run at the same time: data-parallel training across GPUs (gradient all-reduce) or ensembles of independently trained models in parallel; what must be true: enough labeled data that independent models differ and ensemble averaging helps, and GPUs available; nearest source that tried it: Bryant et al. 2021 used ensembles of models (nature URL) — \"all models benefitted from the decision to use ensembles\". \n\ng) For structure-matrix sequential: MSA search (jackhmmer iterations) then AF network recycles (up to 20 in PMC11381518). Runs = pairs × seeds. GPU busy: minutes per prediction. In order within a prediction; across predictions parallel; change: cache MSAs and run seeds/pairs concurrently; must be true: enough GPUs and shared MSA cache; nearest source: PMC11381518 ran 5 models × 20 recycles per pair; or AFDB. Hmm — \"the nearest source that has tried it\" = the nearest source that has tried the parallelization change. For caching MSAs and large-scale prediction: AlphaFold DB (nature s41592-022-01488-1). Let me verify.\n\nh) For endosome-md sequential: MD steps sequential in time (2 fs steps); one run: e.g., 100 ns = 5×10^7 steps; replicas × pH × construct; one GPU per replica (~100% busy); steps must run in order within a trajectory; change: run many independent short trajectories (or replica-exchange) instead of one long one; must be true: the slow relevant motions mix faster than the short trajectory length (so short independent trajectories capture the same ensemble); nearest source that tried many parallel replicas: hmm — need a citable source. Options: (1) the jvi.00843-21 paper (if they ran multiple replicas); (2) \"Anton\" long-trajectory; (3) Folding@home distributed MD — \"Folding@home\" ran many parallel MD; (4) \"HTMD\" or \"OpenMM\". For AAV specifically: maybe the Bryant? no. Let me fetch jvi.00843-21 first and see.\n\nAlso for compute_open of endosome-md: quantity = aggregate simulation time (μs) / replicas. Largest reported by any source I cite: jvi.00843-21's MD lengths (need numbers). Unknown above: whether converging the ensemble would reveal sorting determinants predictive of transcytosis fate (labels only from wet lab).\n\nNow let me fetch jvi.00843-21 and search for AlphaFold DB size and maybe MD throughput numbers (μs/day per GPU) — for \"how much of one GPU a single run keeps busy\" I can just say \"one GPU essentially fully occupied\" without needing a citation... but \"Cite a URL for every factual claim\" — GPU occupancy claims need a source. I could cite a benchmark like Kutzner et al. GronEMES (https://www.cell.com/biophysj/fulltext/S0006-3495(19)30773-8) or OpenMM. Let me search for GPU MD throughput per GPU.\n\nLet me do fetches: jvi.00843-21; search AlphaFold DB 213 million; search GPU MD microsecond per day per GPU."
